{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Do not fear him.</b> It was not fitting for Moses to fear an arm of flesh, for since the Shekhinah was with him, what could a man do to him? After all, the prophet warns Israel (Isaiah 51:12): ״מי את ותיראי מאנוש ימות״ — and the fear of flesh and blood diminishes the soul of the righteous who fears HaShem; and Moses himself warns Israel (Deuteronomy 31:6): ״אל תיראו ואל תערצו מפניהם״, (ibid. v. 1) ״לא תערצון ולא תיראון מהם״. But he feared him because of a merit that he knew he had from the matter of Abraham, and this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded in tractate Niddah, chapter האשה שהיא עושה צריכיה (Niddah 61a): since Sihon and Og were brothers, why did he fear Og and not fear Sihon? From the reply of the Holy One, blessed be He, you recognize what was in the heart of that righteous man — He does not say ״אל תירא״ except to one who is afraid; this teaches that Moses was afraid lest the merit of Abraham stand for him, as it is said (Genesis 14:13): ״ויבא הפליט ויגד לאברם העברי״, and the Master said: this is Og, who escaped from the Flood. And so the Sages, of blessed memory, maintain regarding this Og that he is that same Og of the Flood, and that he reported to Abraham, and lived many days." "<b>And there are those</b> who explained that this Og is not that same one of Abraham himself, but he was of his family and of his seed, and Moses feared him lest the merit of his forefather who lived in the time of Abraham stand for him. And according to this, when they said here that Sihon and Og were brothers — if the intention is brothers literally from the same father and mother, Moses should have feared Sihon as well, for he too would be worthy to take a share in the merit of Og his forefather; but they were brothers from the mother's side, and the main seed of Og the fugitive survived only in this Og, who was of his family and his main seed from the father's side, and therefore Moses feared Og and did not fear Sihon. But we have only what the Sages, of blessed memory, received regarding this Og — that he is literally that same Og of Abraham." "<b>And so</b> they said in the Midrash Tanhuma: ״ויבא הפליט ויגד״ — his intention was so that Abraham would go out and be killed; the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him the reward of his feet, and he lived all those years; and on the other hand, He exacted payment from him, for he fell into the hands of his children. And when Moses came to make war against him, he feared him, saying: I am one hundred and twenty years old, and this one is more than five hundred; were it not that he had merit, he would not have lived all these years. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: ״אל תירא אותו כי בידך נתתי אותו״ — you, with your own hand, kill him, and do to him as you did to Sihon. End." "<b>And one should be roused to ask:</b> since Sihon and Og were brothers before the Flood, and Og alone is the one who escaped, and we see that none were saved except the eight in the ark — if so, from where did this Sihon emerge, and how were they brothers, such that it was said of them that they were brothers?" "<b>And I saw</b> in the Midrash: Sihon and Og were sons of Shemhazai, who was one of the sons of Elohim, and Shemhazai came upon the wife of Ham close to her entry into the ark, and Sihon was born in the ark, and therefore Ham served in the ark in order to cover up for his wife. And according to the Sages, of blessed memory, one must say regarding Sihon that he too lived long like Og his brother, and the longevity of each one was a cause for a certain cause from the Master of causes, blessed be He, and who knows the thoughts of the Maker, may His name be blessed. And what they said in chapter הרואה (Berakhot 54a) — ״מחייה בקרסוליה וקטליה״ (he struck him at the ankle and killed him) — alludes that Moses annulled his merit and the reward of his steps, as the great Rabbi my teacher R. Shem Tov, may his memory be for a blessing, explained in the commentary on the Haggadot that he composed. It follows that you learn that the fear of Moses our master, peace be upon him, was not on account of his strength and might and the height of his stature, but only on account of his merit — for the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, does not cleave to a man according to might and strength but according to merit, as Scripture says (Psalms 147:10–11): ״לא בגבורת הסוס יחפץ לא בשוקי האיש ירצה, רוצה ה' את יראיו את המיחלים לחסדו״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {4 "The abbreviation ר\"ש is resolved as R. Shem Tov based on Rabbeinu Bahya's known reference to R. Shem Tov ibn Gaon as 'הרב הגדול מורי' in his commentary. The closing blessing phrase is reconstructed from the abbreviated שי\"ת."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:04.651435241Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}